<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ethiopian Orthodox Church</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ethiopian Orthodox</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ethiopian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Tewahedo Church is the ancient Christian church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, belonging to the Oriental Orthodox communion. It was founded after the conversion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezana">King Ezana</a> in the fourth century and became a pillar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>, closely linked to the Solomonic monarchy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ethiopian-orthodox-church.jpg" alt="Ethiopian Orthodox Church" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Addis_abeba,_chiesa_della_trinit%C3%A0,_esterno_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Ethiopian Orthodox Church</b>, which traces its institutional lineage directly to this conversion, outlasted the Aksumite dynasty by more than a millennium.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Literacy rose and the <b>Ethiopian Orthodox Church</b> was disestablished in 1974 under a policy of state atheism opposed by most Ethiopians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/derg">Derg · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ethiopian state rested on a double legitimacy, Solomonic royal descent and <b>Ethiopian Orthodox</b> Christian authority: the church gave it ideological coherence, and the emperor claimed sacred kingship from both sources.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The churches have long drawn <b>Ethiopian Orthodox</b> pilgrims, and to some Christians Lalibela is one of Ethiopia's holiest cities.</blockquote><a href="/work/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela">Rock-hewn churches of Lalibela · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1960s the tizita mode became one of the foundations of Ethiopian jazz, or Ethio-jazz, which joined it with elements of <b>Ethiopian Orthodox</b> Christian music and with Western instruments; Mulatu Astatke was its pioneer.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tizita">Tizita</a></li></ul></section>
